(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-emergencycare"]={"plain":{"bills":{"emergencycare":{"slug":"emergencycare","title":"The Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as The Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":null,"body":"The Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** Federal law has required hospitals participating in Medicare to provide an appropriate medical screening examination and stabilizing treatment to any individual who comes to a dedicated emergency department, without regard to the individual's ability to pay, since 1986.\n\n**(2)** That requirement is a condition of a Medicare provider agreement. It therefore does not reach a facility that holds itself out to the public as providing emergency care but does not participate in Medicare, and a member of the public has no means of knowing, from the facility itself, which is which.\n\n**(3)** Because the requirement is a condition of payment, the sanctions available to enforce it are payment sanctions. Between 2005 and 2014, twelve hospitals had a Medicare provider agreement terminated for failing to comply. A sanction that removes a hospital from Medicare harms the patients that hospital serves, and a duty enforced only by a sanction too severe to impose is a duty that is substantially unenforced.\n\n**(4)** Congress has twice established federal quality regulation of a category of health care facility without regard to the payer. The Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments of 1988 require every laboratory examining human specimens to hold a certificate to operate, and impose no direct Medicare or Medicaid program responsibility. The Mammography Quality Standards Act of 1992 requires every mammography facility to be accredited by an approved accreditation body and certified by the Secretary.\n\n**(5)** Those statutes demonstrate that federal certification of a facility type may rest on accreditation performed by approved private nonprofit and State bodies, and that no national inspectorate is required.\n\n**(6)** A State that has imposed equivalent requirements by its own law should not have those requirements displaced or duplicated. The Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments exempt laboratories licensed under an approved State program, and that mechanism is adopted here.\n\n**(7)** The obligation to obtain a certificate before holding oneself out as providing emergency care imposes a duty and confers no payment. Nothing in this Act makes any facility eligible for a payment for which it is not otherwise eligible.\n\n**(8)** Section 1867(g) of the Social Security Act already requires a hospital with specialized capabilities not to refuse an appropriate transfer of an individual requiring those capabilities, if the hospital has the capacity to treat the individual.\n\n**(9)** That duty is substantially unenforced because no record exists against which a claim of lacking capacity or capability can be tested. A physician seeking to transfer a patient telephones one hospital after another, and each may decline for stated lack of capacity with no means by which the statement can be checked.\n\n**(10)** From December 2020 through June 2024, Maryland operated a Critical Care Coordination Center that maintained regional awareness of hospital capability and bed status and was staffed at all hours by a critical care physician and an emergency medical services clinician. It handled approximately 7,000 cases and worked with more than 80 hospitals, including hospitals in four neighboring jurisdictions where Maryland hospitals lacked capacity.\n\n**(11)** In the first six months of that center's operation, 70 percent of the patients it handled had conditions unrelated to the public health emergency during which it was created. The need it met is an ordinary and continuing one.\n\n**(12)** Approximately 27.8 percent of requests to that center were resolved without a transfer, because a specialist advised the requesting clinician how to treat the patient where the patient was. A consultation that prevents an unnecessary transfer avoids an ambulance, a bed, hours of delay, and the movement of a patient away from the patient's family without clinical benefit.\n\n**(13)** That center ceased operating in June 2024 for lack of funding, its prior support having been a public health emergency grant. A service meeting a continuing need should not be financed by a source that expires.\n\n**(14)** Federal systems for coordinating the movement of patients exist and are directed at declared disasters and public health emergencies. They require activation and are not available to a clinician seeking an inpatient bed for a single patient on an ordinary day.\n\n**(15)** Existing Federal reporting of hospital bed census and capacity is required at weekly intervals as a condition of participation in Medicare. That interval is suited to surveillance and is not suited to locating an available bed, and it does not reach whether a clinical service can in fact be delivered."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Ground ambulance \u2014 the surprise bill the No Surprises Act left behind","part":null,"body":"**(b) NO BALANCE BILLING.** \u2014 A provider of ground ambulance services may not bill, or hold liable, an individual for any amount above the cost sharing permitted by subsection (c), and a term of any agreement purporting to permit otherwise is void.\n\n**(c) COST SHARING, CAPPED.** \u2014 The individual's cost sharing may not exceed the lesser of the amount set in the schedule or 10 percent of the recognized amount, **and shall count toward the individual's deductible and out-of-pocket maximum on the same terms as cost sharing for a participating provider.**\n\n**(d) THE RECOGNIZED AMOUNT.** \u2014 For purposes of this section the recognized amount is \u2014\n\n**(1)** where a State, or a unit of local government, has established by law or ordinance a rate for the service furnished, **that rate**; and\n\n**(2)** otherwise, the amount determined under section 7-14, and if that section is not in effect, the percentile set in the schedule of the contracted rates in effect for the same billing code in the same geographic area between plans and participating providers, computed and published by the Secretary.\n\n**(e) LOCAL RATE-SETTING IS PRESERVED, NOT DISPLACED.** \u2014 Nothing in this section limits the authority of a State or unit of local government to set a rate for ground ambulance services it furnishes or franchises, and paragraph (d)(1) applies to that rate as set.\n\n**(f) NO ARBITRATION.** \u2014 No independent dispute resolution, arbitration, or comparable proceeding determines the amount payable under this section, and no party may initiate one.\n\n**(g) PAYMENT OR DENIAL WITHIN 30 DAYS.** \u2014 A plan or issuer shall pay or deny a claim under this section within 30 days of receipt. **An amount not paid or denied within that period accrues interest at the rate set in the schedule, payable to the provider without any request**, and the failure does not transfer any liability to the individual.\n\n**(h) THE BILL MUST BE READABLE.** \u2014 A bill for ground ambulance services shall itemize the services furnished, state the recognized amount and the paragraph of subsection (d) under which it was determined, and state the amount the individual owes and that no other amount may be sought.\n\n**(i) MUNICIPAL AND VOLUNTEER PROVIDERS.** \u2014 This section applies to a provider of ground ambulance services whether operated by a unit of local government, a volunteer organization, a nonprofit entity, or a for-profit entity."},{"anchor":"sec-13-1","heading":"\u00a7 13-1. Definitions","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a) EMERGENCY MEDICAL CONDITION.** \u2014 The term \"emergency medical condition\" has the meaning given that term in section 1867(e)(1) of the Social Security Act. The existence and content of that term as used in this Act do not depend upon the construction any court has given to any provision of the Constitution.\n\n**(b) EMERGENCY FACILITY.** \u2014 The term \"emergency facility\" means a facility that holds itself out as providing emergency care, as determined under section 13-2(b).\n\n**(c) CERTIFIED EMERGENCY FACILITY.** \u2014 The term \"certified emergency facility\" means an emergency facility holding a certificate in effect under section 13-2, or exempt under section 13-5.\n\n**(d) APPROVED ACCREDITATION BODY.** \u2014 The term \"approved accreditation body\" means a private nonprofit organization, or an agency of a State, approved by the Secretary under section 13-4.\n\n**(e) THE NETWORK.** \u2014 The term \"the Network\" means the National Emergency Transfer and Consultation Network established under section 14-2.\n\n**(f) CAPACITY.** \u2014 The term \"capacity\" means the number of beds, by level of care, that are licensed, that are staffed, and that are available for the admission of a patient, each stated separately. A facility has capacity to accept a patient only if it has a bed available for admission at the level of care the patient requires; an available bed at another level of care is not capacity for that patient. This subsection does not construe the term \"capacity\" in section 1867 of the Social Security Act or any regulation issued under it.\n\n**(g) CAPABILITY.** \u2014 The term \"capability\" means whether a clinical service can in fact be delivered by a facility at the time reported, including on-call specialty coverage, operating room availability and interventional capability, without regard to whether the facility is licensed or equipped to deliver that service in general.\n\n**(h) SECRETARY.** \u2014 The term \"Secretary\" means the Secretary of Health and Human Services."},{"anchor":"sec-13-2","heading":"\u00a7 13-2. Certificate required to hold oneself out as providing emergency care","part":null,"body":"**(a) REQUIREMENT.** \u2014 No facility may hold itself out as providing emergency care unless the facility holds a certificate in effect under this section. This subsection applies without regard to whether the facility participates in, or receives payment under, any Federal health care program.\n\n**(b) HOLDING ONESELF OUT.** \u2014 A facility holds itself out as providing emergency care if the facility \u2014\n\n**(1)** uses the term \"emergency,\" \"emergency room,\" \"emergency department,\" \"ER,\" \"ED,\" \"trauma center,\" or any abbreviation or variant of any of them, in its name, signage, advertising, directory listing or other public communication;\n\n**(2)** represents, by any means, that it is available at all hours for the evaluation or treatment of conditions requiring immediate medical attention; or\n\n**(3)** otherwise communicates to the public that it is a place to which a person may present, without an appointment, for the evaluation or treatment of an emergency medical condition.\n\n**(b-1) ILLUSTRATIVE, NOT LIMITING.** \u2014 The enumeration in subsection (b) is illustrative and not limiting.\n\n**(c) ANTI-SUBSTITUTION.** \u2014 Subsection (a) applies to any facility that achieves substantially the result described in subsection (b), regardless of the name, title, designation or characterization the facility uses, regardless of the authority under which the facility is licensed or operates, and regardless of whether the facility describes the care it provides as emergency care, urgent care, immediate care, acute care or by any other term. A change in a facility's name, signage or public communications does not remove the facility from subsection (a) if the facility continues to receive persons presenting without an appointment for the evaluation or treatment of emergency medical conditions.\n\n**(d) NOTICE ALTERNATIVE.** \u2014 A facility is not subject to subsection (a) if the facility \u2014\n\n**(1)** does not use any term described in subsection (b)(1); and\n\n**(2)** posts, at each public entrance and on each page of any public website by which it offers services, a conspicuous notice stating that the facility does not provide emergency care and that a person experiencing a medical emergency should call 911 or go to an emergency room.\n\n**(d-1) EFFECT OF THE NOTICE.** \u2014 A facility that posts the notice required by subsection (d) and thereafter receives a person presenting with what appears to be an emergency medical condition shall provide such care as it is able to provide and arrange transfer under section 14-2, but does not thereby become subject to subsection (a).\n\n**(e) APPLICATION.** \u2014 A certificate shall be issued to any facility that is accredited under section 13-4 and that attests to compliance with section 13-3. The Secretary shall act on a complete application not later than 90 days after receipt. If the Secretary fails to act within that period, the certificate is deemed issued."},{"anchor":"sec-13-3","heading":"\u00a7 13-3. Conditions of the certificate","part":null,"body":"**(a) SCREENING.** \u2014 A certified emergency facility shall provide an appropriate medical screening examination, within the capability of the facility, to any individual who comes to the facility and requests examination or treatment for a medical condition, to determine whether an emergency medical condition exists.\n\n**(b) STABILIZATION AND TRANSFER.** \u2014 Where an emergency medical condition exists, the facility shall provide either such further examination and treatment as is required to stabilize the condition, within the capability of the facility, or transfer of the individual to another facility in accordance with section 14-2.\n\n**(c) WITHOUT REGARD TO.** \u2014 The duties in subsections (a) and (b) apply without regard to the individual's insurance status, ability to pay, method of payment, citizenship, immigration status, or the payer's authorization of the examination or treatment.\n\n**(d) DELAY PROHIBITED.** \u2014 A certified emergency facility may not delay an examination or treatment required by this section in order to inquire about an individual's method of payment or insurance status.\n\n**(e) REPORTING.** \u2014 A certified emergency facility shall report capacity and capability to the Network in accordance with section 14-3. Compliance with section 14-3 is a condition of the certificate.\n\n**(f) SOURCE OF THE DUTY.** \u2014 The duties stated in this section are created by this Act. Their existence and content do not depend upon the construction any court has given to section 1867 of the Social Security Act or to any provision of the Constitution. The defenses available in a proceeding under this section are those and only those provided by this Act; the common law supplies none; and the omission of a defense is a determination of Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-13-4","heading":"\u00a7 13-4. Accreditation and certification","part":null,"body":"**(a) ACCREDITATION REQUIRED.** \u2014 An applicant for a certificate shall be accredited by an approved accreditation body.\n\n**(b) APPROVAL OF BODIES.** \u2014 The Secretary shall approve as an accreditation body any private nonprofit organization, or agency of a State, that demonstrates standards at least as stringent as the conditions in section 13-3 and the capacity to evaluate compliance.\n\n**(c) DEEMED ACCREDITATION.** \u2014 A hospital accredited by an organization whose accreditation the Secretary has recognized for purposes of section 1865 of the Social Security Act is deemed accredited under this section as to any dedicated emergency department it operates, without separate application.\n\n**(d) INSPECTION.** \u2014 An approved accreditation body shall evaluate each facility it accredits not less often than once every three years, and shall evaluate a facility that has not previously held a certificate before accrediting it.\n\n**(e) NO FEDERAL INSPECTORATE.** \u2014 Nothing in this Act authorizes or requires the Secretary to establish a Federal corps of inspectors for facilities certified under this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-13-5","heading":"\u00a7 13-5. Facilities under an approved State program","part":null,"body":"**(a) EXEMPTION.** \u2014 A facility licensed by a State under a program the Secretary has approved under subsection (b) is exempt from sections 13-2 and 13-3, and is a certified emergency facility for all purposes of this Act.\n\n**(b) APPROVAL.** \u2014 The Secretary shall approve a State program on a determination that the State imposes requirements at least as stringent as sections 13-3(a) through (d), applicable to every emergency facility in the State without regard to payer, and enforces them.\n\n**(c) REPORTING NOT EXEMPTED.** \u2014 The exemption in subsection (a) does not extend to section 13-3(e), and a facility exempt under this section shall report to the Network under section 14-3.\n\n**(d) FLOOR, NOT CEILING.** \u2014 Nothing in this Act preempts any State law imposing requirements more stringent than this Act, and no provision of this Act shall be construed to occupy the field of emergency care regulation."},{"anchor":"sec-13-5a","heading":"\u00a7 13-5A. Facilities operated by the United States","part":null,"body":"**(a) EXEMPTION.** \u2014 A facility described in subsection (b) is exempt from sections 13-2 and 13-3, and is not a certified emergency facility.\n\n**(b) FACILITIES COVERED.** \u2014 This section applies to a medical facility of the Department of Veterans Affairs, a military treatment facility, a facility of the Indian Health Service, and a facility operated by an Indian Tribe or a Tribal organization under a contract or compact under the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act.\n\n**(c) THE BOUNDARY THIS PRESERVES.** \u2014 Section 1867 of the Social Security Act binds a hospital that has entered into a provider agreement under section 1866 of that Act, and a facility described in subsection (b) has not. This section preserves that boundary rather than altering it, and states it rather than leaving it to the construction of section 13-2(a). The duties such a facility owes in a medical emergency are those provided by the law governing it, and the omission of any duty under this Act is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d) TRANSFER OUT IS NOT AFFECTED.** \u2014 A facility described in subsection (b) may request identification of facilities and consultation under section 14-2(e), and a coordination center shall serve the request on the same terms as any other request. **A patient whose needs exceed what the facility can provide is the case this Act exists for, and nothing in this section places such a patient outside the Network.**\n\n**(e) REPORTING IS VOLUNTARY, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES A FACILITY FINDABLE.** \u2014 A facility described in subsection (b) may report capacity and capability under section 14-3. While such a report is current the facility is identified under section 14-2(e)(1), and while it is not current the facility is not so identified. Sections 13-6, 14-4 and 14-5 do not apply to a facility described in subsection (b).\n\n**(f) DISASTER AUTHORITIES UNAFFECTED.** \u2014 Nothing in this section limits section 14-9 or any authority exercised on a declaration described in section 14-9(a-2)."},{"anchor":"sec-13-6","heading":"\u00a7 13-6. Enforcement","part":null,"body":"**(a) LADDER.** \u2014 On a determination that a certified emergency facility has failed to comply with section 13-3, the Secretary may, in ascending order of severity \u2014\n\n**(1)** impose conditions on the certificate;\n\n**(2)** suspend the certificate;\n\n**(3)** revoke the certificate; or\n\n**(4)** impose a civil monetary penalty.\n\n**(b) EFFECT OF REVOCATION.** \u2014 A facility whose certificate is revoked may not hold itself out as providing emergency care. Revocation does not affect the facility's participation in any Federal health care program.\n\n**(c) PROPORTIONALITY.** \u2014 In selecting a sanction the Secretary shall consider the seriousness of the failure, whether it was repeated, the size and resources of the facility, and the effect of the sanction on access to care in the area served.\n\n**(d) PENALTY AMOUNT.** \u2014 A civil monetary penalty under subsection (a)(4) shall not exceed the amount set in the schedule, per violation, adjusted annually for inflation.\n\n**(e) REVIEW.** \u2014 A facility may obtain review of a sanction under this section in the United States district court for the district in which it is located."},{"anchor":"sec-13-7","heading":"\u00a7 13-7. Relationship to existing law","part":null,"body":"**(a) SECTION 1867 UNAFFECTED.** \u2014 Nothing in this Act amends, repeals, limits or supersedes section 1867 of the Social Security Act, or any condition of participation under that Act. A hospital participating in Medicare is subject to both that section and this Act.\n\n**(b) NO ELECTION.** \u2014 Compliance with section 1867 of the Social Security Act does not satisfy this Act, and compliance with this Act does not satisfy that section, except that a facility is not liable twice for a single act or omission.\n\n**(c) NO PAYMENT CREATED.** \u2014 Nothing in this Act entitles any facility to payment under any Federal health care program, or makes any facility eligible to enroll in one."},{"anchor":"sec-14-1","heading":"\u00a7 14-1. Definitions","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a) COORDINATION CENTER.** \u2014 The term \"coordination center\" means a regional center participating in the Network under section 14-2(c).\n\n**(b) INPATIENT-LEVEL CARE.** \u2014 The term \"inpatient-level care\" means care requiring admission to a hospital bed, from a general medical or surgical bed through intensive care, and includes emergency surgical care.\n\n**(c) CURRENT.** \u2014 A report of capacity or capability is \"current\" if it was made within the interval required by section 14-3 and has been updated on each event requiring an update under that section.\n\n**(d) AUTOMATED SYSTEM.** \u2014 The term \"automated system\" means a computational process that produces an output bearing on whether a transfer is accepted or declined, or on the discharge of the duty under section 1867(g) of the Social Security Act **not to refuse an appropriate transfer**. An output bears on the discharge of that duty without regard to whether the facility has agreed to accept the transfer within the meaning of section 1867(c)(2)(B)(ii) of that Act; the omission of that element is a determination of Congress and not a gap. The term applies **whatever the method by which the output is produced**, whether or not the process is described as artificial intelligence, whether or not it derives its behavior from data, and whether or not a person could produce the same output by other means.\n\n**(d-1) LICENSED TO PROVIDE INPATIENT-LEVEL CARE.** \u2014 A hospital is licensed to provide inpatient-level care if it holds a license, permit, registration, certificate or other authorization to do so from a State or an Indian Tribe, or if it provides inpatient-level care and the law of a State or Indian Tribe exempts it from licensing by reason of its operation by a State, a unit of local government, an Indian Tribe or a Tribal organization, or an agency or instrumentality of any of them. The State, unit of local government, Indian Tribe or Tribal organization, or agency or instrumentality of any of them, by reason of whose operation a hospital is so exempt is the holder of its license for purposes of this Act, and the holder of an authorization described in the first sentence is the holder of its license. This subsection does not reach a facility described in section 13-5A(b).\n\n**(e) EMERGENCY MEDICAL SERVICES AGENCY.** \u2014 The term \"emergency medical services agency\" means an organization, whether governmental, hospital-based, private or volunteer, that is authorized by a State, a unit of local government or an Indian Tribe, or is operated by a Federal agency, to furnish emergency medical services or interfacility patient transport, by ground, air or otherwise. A hospital or certified emergency facility is an emergency medical services agency only as to the emergency medical services or patient transport it operates.\n\n**(f) HEALTH PLAN, HEALTH INSURANCE ISSUER, AGENT AND AFFILIATE.** \u2014 In section 14-6 \u2014\n\n**(1)** the term \"health plan\" has the meaning given that term in section 1171(5) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1320d(5)), except that a group health plan is a health plan without regard to the number of its participants or the entity that administers it, and a plan that provides only benefits described in section 2791(c)(1)(G) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 300gg-91(c)(1)(G)) is not a health plan. The term also includes, in that capacity only, any person that pays, or decides whether to pay, for the medical care of an individual, including a self-insurer of workers' compensation or liability and a health care sharing ministry, other than that individual, a member of that individual's family or that individual's personal representative; a health care provider or emergency medical services agency is not a health plan under this sentence by reason of paying for care or transport it purchases for a patient it is treating or transporting; no person is an affiliate of a health plan by reason only of its relationship to a person that is a health plan only under this sentence;\n\n**(2)** the term \"health insurance issuer\" has the meaning given that term in section 2791(b)(2) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 300gg-91(b)(2)), whether or not the issuer offers health insurance coverage;\n\n**(3)** an agent of a health plan or health insurance issuer includes any person that performs a function or activity on its behalf, or provides a service to it, involving individually identifiable health information, whether or not that person is its agent under the law of agency, but only while and to the extent that it so acts. A health care provider, or an emergency medical services agency, is not an agent of a health plan or health insurance issuer by reason of furnishing care or transport to its enrollees, or claiming payment from it, under a contract with it;\n\n**(4)** an affiliate of a health plan or health insurance issuer is a person that directly or indirectly controls it, is controlled by it or is under common control with it, a person that holds an ownership or equity interest of 5 percent or more in it, and a person in which it, or a person that controls it, holds such an interest. Control is the power, directly or indirectly, significantly to influence or direct the actions or policies of a person, and includes the power to appoint or remove a majority of a person's governing body, sole or controlling membership in a person, and the direction of a person's management under an agreement. No person controls another by reason only of a contract to furnish, arrange or pay for care, unless the contract confers a power described in the preceding sentence. If the general standard of control stated in this paragraph is held invalid, control means the powers this paragraph names. A person is not an affiliate of a group health plan by reason only that it, or a person under common control with it, established or maintains that plan for its employees. A person is not an affiliate of a health insurance issuer that offers no health insurance coverage and insures only the risks of its owners and of persons that control, are controlled by or are under common control with an owner;\n\n**(5)** a health plan described in subparagraph (D), (E) or (I) through (M) of section 1171(5) of the Social Security Act, or any other plan a government maintains, is the agency administering it, in its capacity administering that plan and in no other. A person that offers coverage or bears risk by contract under such a plan, including under part C or D of title XVIII or title XIX or XXI of that Act or chapter 89 of title 5, United States Code, is a health plan in its own right, except that a health care provider or emergency medical services agency, or an organization of them, is not a health plan, and no person is an affiliate of one, by reason only that it bears risk under a contract to furnish or arrange care or transport. No person is an affiliate of such a plan by reason only of control by, or common control with, a government, and a facility or emergency medical services agency that a government operates, or a clinician a government employs, is not a health plan, or an agent or affiliate of one, by reason of its operator's administration of a plan;\n\n**(6)** a facility described in section 13-5A(b) is not a health plan, or an agent or affiliate of one, by reason of its operation under a program described in subparagraph (I), (J), (K) or (L) of section 1171(5) of the Social Security Act; and\n\n**(7)** the Network, a coordination center and a disaster response system are not, in that capacity, a health plan or an agent or affiliate of one."},{"anchor":"sec-14-2","heading":"\u00a7 14-2. The National Emergency Transfer and Consultation Network","part":null,"body":"**(a) ESTABLISHMENT.** \u2014 The Secretary shall establish and maintain a National Emergency Transfer and Consultation Network.\n\n**(b) SINGLE NUMBER.** \u2014 The Network shall be reachable by a single nationwide telephone number, available at all hours, which shall route the caller to the coordination center serving the caller's location.\n\n**(c) COORDINATION CENTERS.** \u2014 The Network shall operate through regional coordination centers. The Secretary shall align the regions served by coordination centers with the regions of the Regional Disaster Health Response System under section 319C-3(e) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 247d-3c(e)) to the extent practicable, and shall not establish a separate regional geography for the Network where an existing one will serve. An existing geography does not serve where it would not permit the regions to meet subsection (f-9).\n\n**(d) STAFFING.** \u2014 Each coordination center shall be staffed at all hours by at least one physician qualified in critical care or emergency medicine and at least one emergency medical services clinician, and by such nurses, coordinators and other staff as are necessary to carry out the communication and logistics of each request, so that the physician and the emergency medical services clinician direct the clinical coordination of the request.\n\n**(d-1) SPECIALISTS ARE DRAWN FROM HOSPITALS' ON-CALL LISTS.** \u2014 A specialist consulted under subsection (e)(2) shall be a physician on the list a hospital maintains under section 1866(a)(1)(I)(iii) of the Social Security Act, who agrees to provide the consultation. Nothing in this section requires a physician to provide a consultation, and the Network does not pay for one.\n\n**(e) FUNCTIONS.** \u2014 A coordination center shall, on request of a treating clinician \u2014\n\n**(1)** identify facilities having both the capability and the capacity to accept a patient requiring inpatient-level care, whether adult or pediatric, and whether or not the facility is located in the same State as the requesting clinician;\n\n**(2)** provide specialist consultation to the requesting clinician, including, where the question falls outside emergency or critical care medicine, advice the physician on shift obtains from a specialist in the relevant field and relays to the requesting clinician, or a call joining the requesting clinician with that specialist; and\n\n**(3)** where the patient can appropriately be treated where the patient is, advise the requesting clinician accordingly.\n\n**(f) CONSULTATION IS A FUNCTION, NOT A BYPRODUCT.** \u2014 A request resolved under subsection (e)(3) without a transfer is a completed request for all purposes of this Act, including any measure of the Network's performance. No measure of the Network's performance shall treat a transfer as a more successful outcome than a consultation that made a transfer unnecessary.\n\n**(f-1) THE PRIORITY THE NETWORK IS DESIGNED TO SERVE.** \u2014 The Network shall be operated so that a request is answered, and a facility with the necessary capability and capacity identified, most rapidly for the patient whose need is most time-sensitive. Requests shall be prioritized first by the time-sensitivity of the intervention that is unavailable where the patient is, and second by the severity of the patient's illness or injury.\n\n**(f-2) THE ORDER, STATED.** \u2014 In descending priority \u2014\n\n**(1)** an intervention that must be provided without delay to preserve life;\n\n**(2)** an intervention that must be provided without delay to preserve limb, organ or bodily function;\n\n**(3)** a transfer that is not time-sensitive, ordered among such transfers by the severity of the patient's illness or injury.\n\n**(f-3) CLINICAL JUDGMENT GOVERNS THE INDIVIDUAL CASE.** \u2014 Subsections (f-1) and (f-2) state the priority that the Network's protocols and staffing shall reflect. They do not displace the clinical judgment of the physician or other clinician staffing a coordination center, no determination in an individual case is required to conform to them, and no such determination may be set aside on the ground that it did not.\n\n**(f-4) PROTOCOL, PUBLICATION AND RECORD.** \u2014 Each coordination center shall maintain and publish a written protocol reflecting subsections (f-1) and (f-2), and shall record the priority assigned to each request.\n\n**(f-5) NOT AN ALLOCATION AUTHORITY.** \u2014 Nothing in this subsection authorizes a coordination center to accept or decline a transfer, to require a facility to accept a patient, or to displace a patient whose transfer has already been accepted. Section 14-7 applies to every decision described in this subsection.\n\n**(f-6) NO CAUSE OF ACTION FOR A PRIORITY DETERMINATION.** \u2014 A priority determination under this subsection does not create, and shall not be construed to create, a cause of action by or on behalf of any patient. This subsection does not affect any duty or remedy under section 1867 of the Social Security Act, and does not limit any claim arising from a failure to maintain the protocol, publish it, or make the record required by subsection (f-4).\n\n**(f-7) THE MEASURE SUBSECTION (f) ASSUMES, CREATED.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish quarterly, by coordination center: requests offered, answered, and abandoned before a center was reached; **the time from call to a center, and from call to identification of an accepting facility, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile, by priority class under subsection (f-2)**; requests resolved under subsection (e)(3) without a transfer; and the hours in which a center was staffed below subsection (d). Publication is a ministerial duty, enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person.\n\n**(f-8) A SHORTFALL IS NAMED, AND PRICED.** \u2014 Where the times published under subsection (f-7) exceed the periods set in the schedule in more than the share set in the schedule, or a center was staffed below subsection (d) in more than the share set in the schedule, **that pattern is evidence that the Network's staffing is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication**, together with **the physician, emergency medical services clinician, nurse, coordinator and other staff hours required to meet subsection (d) at every hour and those periods,** stating separately the total hours, for the following year, required to staff every coordination center as subsection (d) requires at every hour. The statement is transmitted to the Congress and renewed until the pattern ends.\n\n**(f-9) AND THE NUMBER OF CENTERS IS NOT THE SECRETARY'S ALONE.** \u2014 Subsection (d) states a staffing floor for each center; subsection (c) leaves the number of centers to the Secretary. **A floor of one physician per center, over a denominator the Secretary sets, is not a floor** \u2014 consolidating regions satisfies subsection (d) while lengthening every answer. The regions shall therefore be established, and maintained, so that the periods set in the schedule are met under subsection (f-7), and **a reduction in the number of coordination centers takes effect only after publication of the reduction and of its projected effect on those times.**\n\n**(g) STATE LINES.** \u2014 A coordination center shall identify facilities under subsection (e)(1) without regard to State lines, and shall not exclude a facility from consideration because it is located in another State.\n\n**(h) NO EFFECT ON THE DUTY TO ACCEPT.** \u2014 Use of the Network is not a precondition to any duty under section 1867 of the Social Security Act, and a transfer arranged without the Network is not thereby improper."},{"anchor":"sec-14-3","heading":"\u00a7 14-3. Reporting of capacity and capability","part":null,"body":"**(a) DUTY.** \u2014 Each certified emergency facility, and each hospital licensed to provide inpatient-level care, shall report to the Network its capacity and its capability.\n\n**(b) CAPACITY \u2014 INTERVAL.** \u2014 Capacity shall be reported at the interval specified by the Secretary, which shall not be less frequent than daily, and on each material change in the number of available beds by level of care. Levels of care reported shall distinguish general medical and surgical, telemetry, adult intensive care, pediatric intensive care, neonatal intensive care, and specialty units.\n\n**(c) CAPABILITY \u2014 INTERVAL.** \u2014 Capability shall be reported on each change in whether a clinical service can in fact be delivered, and in any event shall be attested at the interval specified by the Secretary, which shall not be less frequent than weekly.\n\n**(d) TWO CLOCKS, ONE DUTY.** \u2014 The duty under this section is a duty of currency and not a duty of correctness at every instant. A report that was accurate when made and has been updated as required by subsections (b) and (c) satisfies this section.\n\n**(e) PEDIATRIC CAPABILITY.** \u2014 Capability reported under subsection (c) shall separately state pediatric capability.\n\n**(f) MEANS FURNISHED.** \u2014 The Secretary shall furnish, at no charge to the reporting facility, the interface by which reports under this section are made, and shall provide for reporting by a facility that lacks the electronic means to use it.\n\n**(g) NO DUPLICATE REPORTING.** \u2014 The Secretary shall accept a report made under this section in satisfaction of any requirement to report the same data element under title XVIII or XIX of the Social Security Act or any regulation issued under either, including a condition of participation, and shall by regulation conform those requirements so that no facility is required to report the same data element twice."},{"anchor":"sec-14-4","heading":"\u00a7 14-4. Consequence of a report that is not current","part":null,"body":"**(a) FORFEITURE OF THE CAPACITY DEFENSE.** \u2014 A facility whose report of capacity or capability is not current may not rely on a lack of capacity, or a lack of capability, as a basis for declining to accept an appropriate transfer under section 1867(g) of the Social Security Act.\n\n**(a-1) A REFUSAL ITS OWN CURRENT REPORT CONTRADICTS.** \u2014 A facility may not rely on a lack of capacity, or a lack of capability, as a basis for declining to accept an appropriate transfer under section 1867(g) of the Social Security Act where its own current report, as it stood when the facility was asked to accept the transfer, states that capacity or capability. A report made after the request does not change what the report stated for purposes of this subsection. A facility is asked to accept a transfer when the request is first received, by any means, by the facility or by any person acting for it. The consequence in this subsection attaches to the refusal and not to the report. Subsection (b) does not limit this subsection.\n\n**(b) THE VIOLATION IS THE STALE RECORD.** \u2014 Except as subsection (a-1) provides as to a refusal, no consequence under this section attaches to the content of a report. Except as section 14-5(b) and (b-1) provide as to a false report, a facility is not subject to any penalty, condition or sanction under this Act by reason of having reported that it lacks capacity or capability, or by reason of having reported capacity or capability that it is subsequently unable to provide.\n\n**(c) SELF-EXECUTING.** \u2014 The consequences in subsections (a) and (a-1) attach by reason of the state of the record and requires no determination, finding or action by the Secretary or any other officer.\n\n**(d) EXCEPTIONS.** \u2014 No consequence attaches under this section during \u2014\n\n**(1)** a period in which the interface furnished under section 14-3(f) is unavailable; or\n\n**(2)** a declared emergency affecting the facility's ability to report, for the duration of that emergency."},{"anchor":"sec-14-5","heading":"\u00a7 14-5. Further consequences","part":null,"body":"**(a) PUBLICATION.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, not less often than monthly, the proportion of required reports made currently by each facility. Publication under this subsection is a ministerial duty.\n\n**(b) FALSE REPORT.** \u2014 A report that is contradicted by the facility's own contemporaneous records is a false statement and is subject to civil monetary penalty. This subsection and subsection (b-1) are the only provisions of this Act under which a consequence attaches to the content of a report.\n\n**(b-1) A KNOWINGLY FALSE REPORT IS A CRIME.** \u2014 A report under section 14-3 is a statement in a matter within the jurisdiction of the executive branch of the Government of the United States for purposes of section 1001 of title 18, United States Code. Whoever makes, or causes to be made, a report under section 14-3 is subject to that section according to its terms. This subsection does not imply that any other statement made to the Network or a coordination center is outside that jurisdiction. Liability under this subsection reaches each individual who so acts and the facility, and the holder of its license, on whose behalf the report is made, and nothing in this subsection limits the liability of an organization for the acts of its officers, employees or agents under that section.\n\n**(b-2) PERSISTENT FAILURE.** \u2014 A facility persistently fails to comply with section 14-3, for purposes of subsections (c) and (e), in any calendar quarter in which it failed to make currently more than the share set in the schedule of the reports section 14-3 required of it in that quarter. The share is computed on the reports required at the intervals specified under section 14-3(b) and (c); where a report required on a change is shown not to have been made, that report is added both to the reports required and to the reports not made. Each quarter is determined separately on the share computed over the whole quarter, and compliance in one quarter does not offset a failure in another.\n\n**(c) CERTIFICATE LADDER.** \u2014 A persistent failure to comply with section 14-3 is a failure to comply with a condition of the certificate and is addressed under section 13-6. No separate enforcement apparatus is established by this section.\n\n**(d) SCALING.** \u2014 A civil monetary penalty under this section shall be scaled to the size and resources of the facility and shall not exceed the amount set in the schedule for the subsection under which it is imposed. No civil monetary penalty under this section, and no fine imposed for a report described in subsection (b-1), is an amount described in section 162(f)(2)(A) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(e) A HOSPITAL WITH NO CERTIFICATE.** \u2014 The holder of the license of a hospital required to report under section 14-3 that is not a certified emergency facility, where the hospital persistently fails to comply with section 14-3, is subject to a civil monetary penalty for each calendar quarter in which the failure persists, not to exceed, per quarter, the amount set in the schedule. The holder on whom this subsection imposes a penalty includes a State, a unit of local government, an Indian Tribe, a Tribal organization, and any agency or instrumentality of any of them."},{"anchor":"sec-14-6","heading":"\u00a7 14-6. Lawful use and disclosure of reported and transmitted information","part":null,"body":"**(a) CAPACITY AND CAPABILITY DATA ARE NOT PROTECTED HEALTH INFORMATION.** \u2014 Information reported under section 14-3 is not individually identifiable health information and is not protected health information for purposes of the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 or any regulation issued under it.\n\n**(b) TRANSFER AND CONSULTATION DISCLOSURES ARE FOR TREATMENT.** \u2014 A disclosure of individually identifiable health information to a coordination center, to a specialist consulted under section 14-2(e)(2), or to a prospective receiving facility, for the purpose of arranging a transfer or obtaining a consultation, is a disclosure for treatment and may be made without the authorization of the individual.\n\n**(c) NETWORK OPERATIONS.** \u2014 The Network may use information reported under section 14-3, and information obtained under subsection (b), for its operations under section 14-2, including automated analysis to rank, match, surface and forecast the availability of capability and capacity, and may disclose that information, for the same purpose and in connection with a request concerning a specific patient, only to that patient's treating clinician, a facility at which that patient is or that is being asked to accept that patient, a specialist consulted under section 14-2(e)(2) about that patient, or an emergency medical services agency transporting or asked to transport that patient, and only for that patient. A use or disclosure under this subsection is limited to the minimum necessary to accomplish the purpose and is subject to subsection (d), except that the minimum-necessary limit does not apply to a disclosure of information obtained under subsection (b) to a recipient treating or transporting the patient to whom it relates, or to that recipient's use of it for that purpose. A recipient may use and disclose information disclosed to it under this subsection for the transfer, transport or care of that patient, subject to subsection (d), and may not disclose it to a health plan, a health insurance issuer, or an agent or affiliate of either, except in a claim permitted by subsection (d)(1), or in a use or disclosure permitted by subsection (c-2) or (c-4). A ranking or forecast disclosed under this subsection shall state that it is a ranking or forecast and, for each facility it names, the time of that facility's most recent report under section 14-3, and shall state how many facilities whose current report shows the requested capability and capacity it does not name and, for each criterion that excluded any of them, how many it excluded. Each written or electronic disclosure under this subsection shall state that the information is subject to subsection (d) and to the limit on disclosure to a health plan or health insurance issuer stated in this subsection, and the Network shall mark each disclosure under this subsection as made under it. The Secretary shall give each facility and emergency medical services agency standing written notice of that limit. A coordination center may rely on the representation of the requesting clinician, or of a receiving facility, as to who is arranging, providing or receiving the transfer, transport or care. Each disclosure of information obtained under subsection (b) that this section permits is a routine use, from the date this section takes effect and whether or not yet published, for purposes of section 552a of title 5, United States Code, and the Secretary shall publish it as such in the notice required by subsection (e)(4) of that section for any system of records in which the Network maintains that information.\n\n**(c-1) DISASTER RESPONSE SYSTEMS.** \u2014 Information reported under section 14-3 may be disclosed to the disaster response systems defined in section 14-9(a-1), as section 14-9(d) requires, and may be used by those systems to coordinate the movement and care of patients and disclosed by them, for that purpose and in connection with a specific patient, only to that patient's treating clinician, a facility at which that patient is or that is being asked to accept that patient, or an emergency medical services agency transporting or asked to transport that patient. A use, and a disclosure by those systems, under this subsection is limited to the minimum necessary to accomplish the purpose; the disclosure section 14-9(d) requires is not so limited. A recipient of a disclosure by those systems may use and disclose the information for the transfer, transport or care of that patient, and may not disclose it to a health plan, a health insurance issuer, or an agent or affiliate of either, except in a claim permitted by subsection (d)(1), or in a use or disclosure permitted by subsection (c-2) or (c-4). A ranking or forecast disclosed by those systems shall state that it is a ranking or forecast and, for each facility it names, the time of that facility's most recent report under section 14-3, and shall state how many facilities whose current report shows the requested capability and capacity it does not name and, for each criterion that excluded any of them, how many it excluded. Each written or electronic disclosure by those systems under this subsection shall state that the information is subject to subsection (d) and to the limit on disclosure to a health plan or health insurance issuer stated in this subsection, and those systems shall mark each such disclosure as made under this subsection. Those systems may rely on the representation of a requesting clinician, or of a receiving facility, as to who is arranging, providing or receiving the transfer, transport or care. On a transfer of coordination under section 14-9(c), the Network may disclose information obtained under subsection (b) about a patient whose transfer is in progress to the disaster response system taking over coordination of that patient's transfer, which may use and disclose it only for the transfer, transport or care of that patient, limited to the minimum necessary, and subject to the same limits on onward disclosure as a recipient under subsection (c). A use or disclosure under this subsection is subject to subsection (d) notwithstanding section 14-9(a), and section 14-9(a) supplies no further use or disclosure.\n\n**(c-2) ENFORCEMENT OF THIS ACT.** \u2014 Notwithstanding subsection (d), information a facility reported under section 14-3, and the times at which the Network received it, may be used and disclosed to determine, as to that facility \u2014\n\n**(1)** whether its report was current, and what its current report stated, for purposes of section 14-4;\n\n**(2)** whether its report was false under section 14-5(b) or (b-1); and\n\n**(3)** whether it failed to comply with section 14-3, for purposes of sections 13-6, 14-5(b-2), 14-5(c), and 14-5(e);\n\nand may be used and disclosed in a proceeding to determine that question: for paragraph (1), any proceeding in which the facility relies on a lack of capacity or capability, including a proceeding under section 1128A, 1866(b)(2) or 1867 of the Social Security Act; for paragraph (2), a proceeding to impose a penalty under section 14-5(b) or to review its imposition, a prosecution under section 1001 of title 18, United States Code, of a report described in section 14-5(b-1), or under section 371 or 1519 of that title in connection with such a report, and an investigation to determine whether to bring that prosecution; and for paragraph (3), a proceeding under section 13-6, or a proceeding to impose a penalty under section 14-5(e) or to review its imposition. A use or disclosure under this subsection is limited to the question stated in it. Except in a proceeding described for paragraph (1) or (2), the content of a report may not be used under this subsection as evidence of a facility's capacity or capability; using the content of a report to establish what the facility reported, for comparison with the facility's own records, is not such a use. On the request of a party to a proceeding described in this subsection, the Secretary shall furnish to that party the facility's reports under section 14-3 for the period the request states and the times at which the Network received them, and shall certify whether what is furnished is complete; furnishing and certifying are ministerial duties owed to that party, enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by that party. This subsection permits no use or disclosure of information obtained under subsection (b).\n\n**(c-3) USES THIS ACT DIRECTS THE SECRETARY TO MAKE.** \u2014 The Secretary may use information reported under section 14-3, and the times at which the Network received it \u2014\n\n**(1)** to accept a report under section 14-3(g) in satisfaction of a requirement to report the same data element under title XVIII or XIX of the Social Security Act or a regulation issued under either, including a condition of participation, and to use and disclose the data element so accepted for any purpose for which a report made under that requirement could be used or disclosed on the date of enactment of this Act, including to determine whether the facility satisfied the requirement; and\n\n**(2)** to compute and publish under section 14-5(a) the proportion of required reports each facility made currently.\n\nA use or disclosure under paragraph (1) is subject to subsection (d), except that determining whether a facility satisfied a requirement described in that paragraph, and a proceeding under section 1866(b)(2) of the Social Security Act to address a failure to satisfy it, are not a purpose described in paragraph (1) or (3) of subsection (d). This subsection permits no use or disclosure of information obtained under subsection (b).\n\n**(c-4) ENFORCEMENT OF THE TRANSFER DUTY.** \u2014 Notwithstanding subsection (d)(3), information obtained under subsection (b) may be used and disclosed, limited to the minimum necessary, in a proceeding under section 1128A, 1866(b)(2) or 1867 of the Social Security Act to determine whether a facility complied with section 1867(g) of that Act, and in an investigation to determine whether to bring such a proceeding, and in a report that section 1866 or 1867 of that Act, or a regulation implementing either, requires a hospital to make. No use or disclosure under this subsection may be made for any immigration purpose.\n\n**(c-5) INFORMATION KEPT FROM PAYERS.** \u2014\n\n**(1)** A health plan, a health insurance issuer, or an agent of either acting as such, may not request, obtain, use or disclose information disclosed under subsection (c) or (c-1) except as subsection (c-2), (c-4) or (d)(1) permits, except that a health plan or health insurance issuer that is itself a recipient under subsection (c) or (c-1) may use and disclose the information as that subsection permits a recipient, subject to paragraph (2).\n\n**(2)** A recipient under subsection (c) or (c-1) that is, or controls, a health plan or health insurance issuer, or that is an agent of either, may not use information disclosed to it under that subsection for, or make it available to any person performing, payment, coverage, eligibility, rating, underwriting, utilization review or plan administration, except as subsection (d)(1) permits.\n\n**(3)** A person acting for a recipient under subsection (c) or (c-1) may not, in any capacity, use or disclose the information for, or make it available to, a health plan, a health insurance issuer, or an agent or affiliate of either, except as that subsection permits the recipient.\n\n**(4)** If the limit on disclosure to an affiliate in subsection (c) or (c-1) is held invalid as applied to a disclosure, for the care or transport of the patient to whom the information relates, to a health care provider or emergency medical services agency that is an affiliate of a health plan or health insurance issuer, the disclosure is permitted, and the affiliate is a recipient under that subsection, and paragraph (2) applies to it as though it controlled that health plan or health insurance issuer.\n\n**(d) PERMITTED USES ARE CLOSED.** \u2014 Information reported under section 14-3 or obtained under subsection (b) may not be used or disclosed \u2014\n\n**(1)** for payment, or for any coverage, eligibility, rating or underwriting determination, except that a facility or emergency medical services agency may use information disclosed to it under subsection (b) or (c) to claim payment for care or transport it furnished to the patient to whom the information relates, and a health plan or health insurance issuer, or an agent of either acting for it, may use the information in that claim to determine that claim and no other; a consultation under section 14-2(e)(2) is not care furnished for purposes of this paragraph;\n\n**(2)** for utilization review adverse to a patient;\n\n**(3)** for any law enforcement or immigration purpose, except a use or disclosure permitted by subsection (c-2) or (c-4); or\n\n**(4)** for sale, licensing or transfer for value, including the sale, licensing or transfer for value of any compilation, forecast or product derived from it.\n\nThis subsection, and each other limit this section places on a recipient, on a person acting for one, or on a health plan, a health insurance issuer or an agent of either, binds every person holding, requesting or obtaining the information, including a State, a unit of local government, an Indian Tribe, and any agency or instrumentality of any of them, except that this section does not govern a facility's use or disclosure of the information that facility itself reported under section 14-3, as held by that facility.\n\n**(d-1) CLOSURE.** \u2014 The uses and disclosures permitted by this section are those and only those stated in it. No other authority, whether or not enacted after this Act, supplies a further permitted use or disclosure unless it amends this section by express reference. The omission of any use or disclosure from this section is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d-1A) WHAT A RECIPIENT LEARNS FOR ITSELF.** \u2014 Information a facility, clinician or emergency medical services agency creates or obtains by its own examination, treatment or transport of a patient is not information obtained under subsection (b) or disclosed under subsection (c) by reason only that the patient came to it through the Network, and this section does not govern its use or disclosure.\n\n**(d-2) SUBSTANCE USE DISORDER AND OTHER PROTECTED RECORDS.** \u2014 Nothing in this section authorizes a use or disclosure of a record subject to section 543 of the Public Health Service Act, or to section 7332 of title 38, United States Code, that the section governing it does not permit."},{"anchor":"sec-14-7","heading":"\u00a7 14-7. Decisions are made by persons","part":null,"body":"**(a) PROHIBITION.** \u2014 No automated system may accept or decline a transfer, and no automated system may satisfy, discharge or excuse the duty under section 1867(g) of the Social Security Act to accept an appropriate transfer.\n\n**(b) ATTRIBUTION.** \u2014 A decision to decline a transfer shall be made by a natural person, recorded as having been made by that person, and attributable to that person by name and role.\n\n**(c) WHAT A SYSTEM MAY DO.** \u2014 Nothing in this section limits the use of an automated system to rank, surface, match, alert, forecast demand, or inform a clinician which facilities are likely to have a given capability or capacity.\n\n**(d) NO WAIVER.** \u2014 The requirement of this section may not be waived by agreement, by protocol, or by a facility's internal policy."},{"anchor":"sec-14-8","heading":"\u00a7 14-8. Individuals in the custody of a State","part":null,"body":"**(a) MODEL PROTOCOL.** \u2014 The Secretary, in consultation with the Attorney General, shall publish a model protocol for the interstate transfer of an individual in the custody of a State who requires inpatient-level care that the nearest facility with capability and capacity can provide only in another State. The protocol shall address the transfer of temporary custody, the authority of the receiving State during the hospitalization, security arrangements, notification, and return.\n\n**(b) NO NEW COMPACT AND NO NEW CONSENT.** \u2014 The model protocol is offered for adoption by States under authority they already possess, including the Interstate Corrections Compact and the consent given by the Act of June 6, 1934. Nothing in this section creates a compact, requires a State to enter one, or requests the consent of Congress to any agreement between States.\n\n**(c) PAYMENT WITHOUT SEPARATE ENROLLMENT.** \u2014 A hospital furnishing inpatient services to an individual in the custody of a State other than the State in which the hospital is located shall be paid under that State's plan under title XIX of the Social Security Act, where payment is otherwise available, without being required to enroll separately as a provider in that State's program.\n\n**(d) NETWORK INTEGRATION.** \u2014 A coordination center shall record which facilities in its region are able to accept an individual in custody, and shall not exclude such an individual from the identification of facilities under section 14-2(e)(1).\n\n**(e) NO CONDITION ON FUNDS.** \u2014 No payment to a State under any Federal program is conditioned on the State's adoption of the model protocol, and adoption is voluntary."},{"anchor":"sec-14-9","heading":"\u00a7 14-9. Relationship to disaster response systems","part":null,"body":"**(a) NO DISPLACEMENT.** \u2014 Except as subsection (e) provides, nothing in this Act limits, replaces or supersedes any disaster response system, or any authority exercised on a declaration described in subsection (a-2).\n\n**(a-1) DISASTER RESPONSE SYSTEM DEFINED.** \u2014 In this section and section 14-6, the term \"disaster response system\" means \u2014\n\n**(1)** the National Disaster Medical System under section 2812 of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 300hh-11);\n\n**(2)** the Regional Disaster Health Response System under section 319C-3(e) of that Act (42 U.S.C. 247d-3c(e)), and each of its regions;\n\n**(3)** a medical operations coordination center recognized under section 319C-3(f) of that Act; and\n\n**(4)** an agency of a State, a unit of local government or an Indian Tribe that coordinates the movement of patients among facilities on a declaration described in subsection (a-2); an agency that performs that function only on such a declaration is such an agency between declarations.\n\n**(a-2) DECLARATION.** \u2014 A declaration described in this subsection is \u2014\n\n**(1)** a declaration by the President of a major disaster or an emergency under section 401 or 501 of the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (42 U.S.C. 5170, 5191);\n\n**(2)** a determination by the Secretary under section 319 of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 247d) that a public health emergency exists; or\n\n**(3)** a declaration of a disaster, an emergency or a public health emergency by the Governor of a State, or by the chief executive or governing body of an Indian Tribe, under the law of that State or Indian Tribe.\n\n**(b) EVERYDAY OPERATION.** \u2014 The Network operates without any declaration, activation or emergency determination.\n\n**(c) HANDOFF.** \u2014 The Secretary shall provide by regulation for the transfer of coordination from the Network to the applicable disaster response system on a declaration described in subsection (a-2), and for its return when that declaration ends. The applicable disaster response system is a disaster response system that has been activated on that declaration, or is coordinating the movement of patients under it, for the area concerned; where there is none, the Network retains coordination. Until the regulation takes effect, the Network retains coordination. Coordination passes to a disaster response system that is, or is operated by, a State, a unit of local government, an Indian Tribe or a Tribal organization, or an agency or instrumentality of any of them, only with its agreement; absent that agreement, the Network retains coordination.\n\n**(d) SHARED INFORMATION.** \u2014 The Secretary shall make information reported under section 14-3 available to the disaster response systems as it is reported, by the means and in the form the Network uses for its own operations. Making it available is a disclosure permitted by section 14-6(c-1) and is a ministerial duty, owed to each disaster response system described in subsection (a-1)(3) or (4) and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by it.\n\n**(e) OUTSIDE A DECLARATION, A REGIONAL SYSTEM SEES ITS OWN REGION.** \u2014 Except while a declaration described in subsection (a-2) is in effect for any part of the area a disaster response system described in subsection (a-1)(3) or (4) serves, the information made available to that system under subsection (d) is limited to information reported by facilities located in that area and in the areas adjacent to it. For a medical operations coordination center, that area is the area stated in its recognition under section 319C-3(f) of the Public Health Service Act."},{"anchor":"sec-14-9a","heading":"\u00a7 14-9A. The disaster response systems, in permanent law","part":null,"body":"**(a) REGIONAL DISASTER HEALTH RESPONSE SYSTEM.** \u2014 Section 319C-3(e) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 247d-3c(e)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(e) ~~Demonstration project for regional health care preparedness and response systems~~ Regional Disaster Health Response System**\n>\n> **(1) In general**\n>\n> The Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response ~~may establish a demonstration project pursuant to the development and implementation of guidelines under subsection (b) to award grants~~ **shall maintain, consistent with the guidelines under subsection (b) where issued, a Regional Disaster Health Response System, organized in regions the Assistant Secretary establishes, and may award grants under it** to improve medical surge capacity for all hazards, build and integrate regional medical response capabilities, improve specialty care expertise for all-hazards response, and coordinate medical preparedness and response across State, local, Tribal, territorial, and regional jurisdictions.\n>\n> ~~**(2) Sunset** The authority under this subsection shall expire on September 30, 2023.~~\n>\n> **(2) Authorization of appropriations**\n>\n> **There are authorized to be appropriated to carry out this subsection such sums as may be necessary for each fiscal year. Amounts appropriated under this paragraph are in addition to any amount reserved under section 319C-2(j)(1)(B).**\n\n**(a-1) THE RESERVATION FOR REGIONAL SYSTEMS DOES NOT EXPIRE.** \u2014 Section 319C-2(j)(1)(B) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 247d-3b(j)(1)(B)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(B) Reservation of amounts for regional systems**\n>\n> **(i) In general** Subject to clause (ii), of the amount appropriated under subparagraph (A) for a fiscal year, the Secretary may reserve up to 5 percent for the purpose of carrying out section 319C-3.\n>\n> **(ii) Reservation contingent on continued appropriations for this section** If for fiscal year 2019 or a subsequent fiscal year, the amount appropriated under subparagraph (A) is such that, after application of clause (i), the amount remaining for the purpose of carrying out this section would be less than the amount available for such purpose for the previous fiscal year, the amount that may be reserved under clause (i) shall be reduced such that the amount remaining for the purpose of carrying out this section is not less than the amount available for such purpose for the previous fiscal year.\n>\n> ~~**(iii) Sunset** The authority to reserve amounts under clause (i) shall expire on September 30, 2023.~~\n\n**(b) MEDICAL OPERATIONS COORDINATION CENTERS.** \u2014 Section 319C-3 of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 247d-3c) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(f) Medical operations coordination centers**\n>\n> **(1) Recognition.** The Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response shall recognize as a medical operations coordination center any entity that applies for recognition, that is operated by or under an agreement with a State, a unit of local government, an Indian tribe or a tribal organization (as those terms are defined in section 4 of the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act (25 U.S.C. 5304)), a health care coalition under section 319C-2(b), or a region of the Regional Disaster Health Response System under subsection (e), and that coordinates the movement of patients among hospitals and health care facilities to balance the load of patients among them during, in advance of, or immediately following a public health emergency, a disaster or an emergency. The recognition shall state the area the entity serves, as the entity states it in its application, which may not exceed the combined jurisdictions of the States, units of local government and Indian tribes, the areas in which the tribal organizations provide or arrange for health services, and the areas of the health care coalitions and regions, by which or under agreement with which the entity is operated. The Assistant Secretary shall act on an application not later than 30 days after receipt; an application not acted on within that period is deemed granted, stating the area the application states to the extent it does not exceed that limit. Recognition is a ministerial act and continues until the entity ceases to perform that function, is no longer operated by or under an agreement with any body named in the first sentence, or withdraws, and the area it states may not at any time exceed that limit as it then stands. A body's written notice to the Assistant Secretary that it no longer operates the entity, or that its agreement with the entity has ended, is conclusive for this paragraph. An entity that performs that function only on activation does not cease to perform it between activations. The Assistant Secretary shall publish, and keep current, a list of the entities recognized under this paragraph, stating for each the bodies by which or under agreement with which it is operated and the area its recognition states.\n>\n> **(2) No authority conferred over facilities.** Recognition under this subsection confers no authority to require a hospital or health care facility to accept a patient or to report information. Any such authority a medical operations coordination center holds is that given by the law of the State or Indian tribe concerned or by agreement.\n\n**(c) NATIONAL DISASTER MEDICAL SYSTEM.** \u2014 Section 2812(g) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 300hh-11(g)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(g) Authorization of appropriations**\n>\n> For the purpose of providing for the Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response and the operations of the National Disaster Medical System, other than purposes for which amounts in the Public Health Emergency Fund under section 319 are available, there are authorized to be appropriated $57,400,000 for ~~each of fiscal years 2019 through 2023~~ **fiscal year 2019 and each fiscal year thereafter**.\n\n**(d) NATIONAL DISASTER MEDICAL SYSTEM APPOINTMENTS.** \u2014 Section 2812(c)(4) of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 300hh-11(c)(4)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(4) Certain appointments**\n>\n> ~~**(A) In general**~~ If the Secretary determines that the number of intermittent disaster response personnel within the National Disaster Medical System under this section is insufficient to address a public health emergency or potential public health emergency, the Secretary may appoint candidates directly to personnel positions for intermittent disaster response within such system. The Secretary shall provide updates on the number of vacant or unfilled positions within such system to the congressional committees of jurisdiction each quarter for which this authority is in effect.\n>\n> ~~**(B) Sunset** The authority under this paragraph shall expire on December 31, 2026.~~"},{"anchor":"sec-14-10","heading":"\u00a7 14-10. Funding","part":null,"body":"**(a) CHARGE IMPOSED.** \u2014 There is imposed an annual charge, in the amount determined under subsection (b), on the holder of the license of each hospital licensed to provide inpatient-level care, on the holder of the State license of each certified emergency facility, and, for a certified emergency facility that is not licensed by a State, on the person operating it. A person to whom more than one of those descriptions applies as to the same hospital or facility is charged once, and no licensed bed or emergency department visit is counted in more than one charge. For purposes of this section, \"person\" includes a State, a unit of local government, an Indian Tribe, a Tribal organization, and any agency or instrumentality of any of them. No charge is imposed as to a hospital or facility operated by an Indian Tribe or a Tribal organization under a contract or compact under the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act that has not entered into an agreement under section 1866 of the Social Security Act.\n\n**(a-1) PERSONS UNDER COMMON CONTROL.** \u2014\n\n(1) Each person that, at any time during the year for which the charge is imposed, is in the same controlled group as the person on whom subsection (a) imposes the charge is jointly and severally liable for the charge.\n\n(2) Two persons are in the same controlled group if \u2014\n\n(A) they are treated as a single employer under subsection (a) or (b) of section 52, or subsection (b), (c), (m) or (o) of section 414, of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, with section 414(c)(2) of that Code, and any regulation under section 414 of that Code excluding or separately treating a church or a qualified church-controlled organization (as defined in section 3121(w)(3) of that Code), disregarded, and with section 1563 of that Code applied without regard to subsection (b) thereof;\n\n(B) one of them, alone or together with persons to which it stands in a relation described in this subparagraph \u2014\n\n(i) is the sole member of the other;\n\n(ii) holds more than 50 percent of the votes the other's members are entitled to cast;\n\n(iii) has the power to appoint or remove a majority of the other's governing body; or\n\n(iv) has, among the individuals serving on the other's governing body, a majority who are its officers or who serve by reason of an office or position in it or by its designation or nomination;\n\n(C) they are connected through a chain of persons, each in the same controlled group as the next under subparagraph (B), or under subparagraph (A) otherwise than by reason of section 414(m) or (o) of that Code; or\n\n(D) one of them furnishes, by lease or otherwise, property or services used in operating a hospital or facility as to which subsection (a) imposes the charge on the other, and they are related within the meaning of section 707(b)(1)(B), or paragraph (10), (11) or (12) of section 267(b), of that Code.\n\nA right held only as a creditor and exercisable only on the debtor's default counts under subparagraph (B) only from the time it is exercised, and a right held only by reason of a government's regulatory authority, or of an appointment by a court or government agency as receiver, trustee or conservator, does not count under subparagraph (B). This paragraph applies to a church or a qualified church-controlled organization as it applies to any other organization in the same relation, and no two persons are in the same controlled group by reason only of a shared religious affiliation, belief or practice.\n\n(3) No State, unit of local government, Indian Tribe or Tribal organization, and no agency or instrumentality of any of them, is liable under this subsection for a charge imposed on another person, and two persons are not in the same controlled group by reason only that one such government, agency or instrumentality is in the same controlled group as each of them under paragraph (2).\n\n(4) A person is liable under paragraph (1) only for the part of each charge for a year proportionate to the days of that year it was in the controlled group if, during that year, it ceased to be in the group because any interest, membership, votes or appointment power by reason of which it was in the group passed, for fair market value or for no consideration, to a person that, immediately before the passing, was not in the group and was not related to any person then in it within the meaning of section 267(b) or 707(b)(1) of that Code, and it was not again in the group later in that year. A person in the group only under paragraph (2)(D) that ceases during a year to furnish the property or services described there, and does not furnish them again later in that year, is liable under paragraph (1) only for the part of each charge for that year proportionate to the days it furnished them.\n\n(5) No individual is liable under this subsection.\n\n(6) The return required by subsection (d) shall state the name and taxpayer identification number of each person in the same controlled group as the person making it at any time during the year, the days of the year each was in it, and any passing described in paragraph (4). An assessment of the charge against the person on whom subsection (a) imposes it is an assessment against each person the return states of the amount for which paragraph (1), as limited by paragraph (4) applied to the days and passings the return states, makes that person liable, and any further amount for which that person is liable may be assessed against it within the period section 6501 of that Code allows; and notice and demand under section 6303 of that Code shall be given to each. For purposes of section 6501 of that Code, that return is the return of each person it states. A person liable under paragraph (1) whom the return does not state may file with the Secretary of the Treasury a statement, made under penalties of perjury, naming the person on whom subsection (a) imposes the charge and stating as to itself what this paragraph requires the return to state; for purposes of section 6501 of that Code, the statement is its return, filed on the day it is filed or, if the return required by subsection (d) is filed later, on that day. The charge may be assessed at any time against a person liable under paragraph (1) whom the return does not state and that has filed no such statement.\n\n(7) A person that pays under paragraph (1) any part of a charge imposed on another person is subrogated to the claim of the United States against that person to the extent of the payment and has a right of contribution from it.\n\n(8) No compromise, release, extension of time or other agreement with, or discharge of, a person liable for a charge discharges or reduces the liability of any other person for that charge, except to the extent of an amount paid.\n\n(9) If collection of the charge, or of a liability for it under paragraph (1) or subsection (a-2), from property used for religious worship is held invalid, or is held barred by the Religious Freedom Restoration Act of 1993, the charge or liability is not affected and may be collected from any other property of the person liable.\n\n**(a-2) CHANGE OF HOLDER DURING A YEAR.** \u2014 Where the license of a hospital or facility, or its operation, passes from one person to another during a year, the charge for that hospital or facility for that year is divided between them in proportion to the days of the year each held it, and the person acquiring it is jointly and severally liable for any charge as to that hospital or facility that remains unpaid on the date of acquisition.\n\n**(b) COMPUTATION.** \u2014 The charge shall be computed on the basis of licensed beds and emergency department visits, in the proportions set in the schedule, so that the aggregate charge collected equals the appropriations required to operate the Network. **The appropriations required to operate the Network are computed to meet section 14-2(d) at every hour, including the nurses, coordinators and other staff it requires, and the periods set in the schedule under section 14-2(f-8), and the computation is published with the charge.** Each computation includes at least the cost, at the rates the Network pays, of the total hours most recently stated under section 14-2(f-8) as required to staff every coordination center as section 14-2(d) requires at every hour.\n\n**(b-1) EMERGENCY MEDICAL SERVICES AGENCIES ARE NOT CHARGED.** \u2014 No charge is imposed under this section on an emergency medical services agency, except as a person on whom subsection (a) imposes the charge, and no payment under section 6-2(a)(4) may be reduced or offset by reason of the agency's use of the Network. No licensed bed or emergency department visit is excluded from the base under subsection (b) by reason that the hospital or facility, or a person operating or affiliated with it, is an emergency medical services agency.\n\n**(c) BASE MAY NOT KEY ON TRANSFERS.** \u2014 The charge may not be computed by reference to the number of transfers a facility makes, receives or requests, or to the number of requests made to the Network. A reduction in transfers resulting from the operation of section 14-2(e)(3) shall not reduce the base on which the charge is computed.\n\n**(d) TAX CHARACTER.** \u2014 The charge imposed by this section is a tax, is reported and paid by the person on whom subsection (a) imposes it, on a return made in the form and at the time the Secretary of the Treasury prescribes, and is an ordinary and necessary business expense of that person. It is not imposed by reason of, and does not require any finding of, a violation of law, and section 162(f) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 does not apply to it. For purposes of subtitle F of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, the charge shall be treated as if it were a tax imposed by that Code. Notwithstanding the second sentence of section 6331(b) of that Code, the effect of a levy to collect the charge, or a liability for it under subsection (a-1) or (a-2), on a right to payment for medical care furnished at any hospital or facility as to which a charge under this section is imposed on any person is continuous from the date the levy is first made until it is released under section 6343 of that Code.\n\n**(e) PERMANENT APPROPRIATION.** \u2014 Amounts collected under this section are appropriated to the Secretary for the operation of the Network, without fiscal year limitation, and remain available until expended.\n\n**(f) NO LAPSE.** \u2014 The functions of the Network are excepted functions and continue during any lapse in appropriations. **The officers and employees necessary to perform the functions of the coordination centers, including the physicians, emergency medical services clinicians, nurses, coordinators and other staff section 14-2(d) requires on shift, are exempt from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, being paid from the appropriation made by subsection (e); any of them not so paid shall be designated as excepted from furlough; and each shall be identified as such in the published shutdown plan of the agency concerned.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-6","heading":"\u00a7 0-6. References to sections of related Acts","part":null,"body":"**(a) SCOPE.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to a section not contained in this Act is a reference to that section of the One Medicare Act, the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act, the Long-Term Care Act, the Public Option Act, the Lowest Drug Price Wins Act, the Medical Care PAID Act, the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, and the Patient Safety and Compensation Act, as the context requires.\n\n**(b) IF THE RELATED ACT IS NOT IN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a section referred to under subsection (a) is not in effect, the reference applies as that section read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(c) IF THE SECTION HAS NEVER TAKEN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a provision of this Act conditions a consequence on a report, program, payment, or determination established by a section not contained in this Act, and that section has never taken effect, **the condition is inapplicable and the remainder of the provision operates according to its terms.**\n\n**(d) THIS ACT STANDS ALONE.** \u2014 No provision of this Act depends for its operation on the enactment of any other Act, and the failure of any other Act to be enacted does not affect the operation of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7","heading":"\u00a7 0-7. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or the application of any provision to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and the application of the provision to any other person or circumstance are not affected.\n\n**(b) NOT ONE INTEGRATED SCHEME.** \u2014 The provisions of this Act are separate and independent. **A court shall not treat this Act as a single integrated scheme, and shall not infer from the invalidity of one provision that the Congress would not have enacted the remainder.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-8","heading":"\u00a7 0-8. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a) IN GENERAL.** \u2014 Except as otherwise provided, this Act takes effect on the first day of the first plan year beginning after the date that is one year after the date of enactment.\n\n**(b) SCHEDULE VALUES.** \u2014 Where a provision refers to an amount, period, threshold, or rate set in the schedule, that value is the value stated in the SCHEDULE to this Act."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 7-6(c) cost-sharing amount | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 7-6(d)(2) percentile of contracted rates | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 7-6(g) interest rate on an amount not paid or denied within 30 days | the rate used for purposes of section 3902(a) of title 31, United States Code | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 13-6(d) maximum civil monetary penalty per violation | $136,886; $68,445 for a facility with fewer than 100 beds; adjusted for inflation from calendar year 2025 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7\u00a7 14-2(f-8), (f-9), 14-10(b) answer period, from call to a coordination center, for every priority class under \u00a7 14-2(f-2) | 30 seconds | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7\u00a7 14-2(f-8), (f-9), 14-10(b) identification period, from call to identification of an accepting facility, priority class \u00a7 14-2(f-2)(1) | 30 minutes | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7\u00a7 14-2(f-8), (f-9), 14-10(b) identification period, priority class \u00a7 14-2(f-2)(2) | 30 minutes | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7\u00a7 14-2(f-8), (f-9), 14-10(b) identification period, priority class \u00a7 14-2(f-2)(3) | 30 minutes | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 14-2(f-8) share of a center's requests in a quarter, in a priority class, whose time exceeds the period in S-5, S-6, S-7 or S-8 that constitutes a pattern | 10 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 14-2(f-8) share of a center's hours in a quarter staffed below \u00a7 14-2(d) that constitutes a pattern | 5 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 14-5(b), (d) maximum civil monetary penalty | $127,973 per false report | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 14-10(b) proportions of licensed beds and emergency department visits in the charge computation | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 14-5(e) maximum civil monetary penalty per calendar quarter | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 14-5(b-2) share of the reports required in a calendar quarter that, if not made currently, constitutes a persistent failure | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-18"}}}};
